# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your real values.
#
# The same four required values go into your hosting provider's environment
# settings (on Vercel: Project → Settings → Environment Variables). .env.local
# is git-ignored and never leaves your machine, so it does NOT reach production.
#
# ── REQUIRED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# 1. Your WordPress site's Kandi API base, with no trailing slash.
#    Everything the shop knows — products, orders, sellers, payments — comes
#    through here.
WP_API_URL=https://shop.kandiug.com/wp-json/kandi/v1


# 2. The shared secret, matching the one in WordPress.
#    Set it in wp-config.php as `define( 'KANDI_API_SECRET', '…' );` or under
#    wp-admin → Kandi Storefront → Storefront connection. Every order, seller
#    and payment request carries it, so only your shop can reach those
#    endpoints. Make it long and random.
KANDI_API_SECRET=


# 3. The storefront's public URL, no trailing slash — e.g. https://kandiug.com
#
#    Pesapal builds its callback from it, and SEARCH ENGINES NEED IT MORE:
#    every canonical tag, every URL in sitemap.xml and every Open Graph image is
#    built from this value. Left blank outside Vercel it falls back to
#    http://localhost:3000, and the live site then tells Google its real pages
#    are duplicates of an address nobody can reach — indistinguishable from
#    asking not to be indexed at all.
#
#    Vercel fills this in by itself from the deployment URL. On a custom domain,
#    or any other host, set it by hand and check https://your-site/sitemap.xml
#    afterwards: if the URLs in it say localhost, this is why.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://kandiug.com


# 4. Google sign-in. Create an OAuth 2.0 Web client at
#    https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials and add BOTH origins to
#    "Authorised JavaScript origins" — http://localhost:3000 and your live
#    domain — or the button fails on whichever is missing.
#
#    Without this, shopper sign-in, the account dashboard, seller Google
#    sign-in and writing reviews are all unavailable.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=


# ── OPTIONAL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# Better address suggestions at checkout.
#
#    WHAT WORKS WITHOUT IT
#      Everything. With no key the checkout's location box suggests as you type
#      using OpenStreetMap's Nominatim — free, no account, no card — and the
#      delivery price is calculated from coordinates either way. This is the
#      only paid service the shop can use, and it is optional.
#
#    WHAT THE KEY BUYS
#      Google is markedly better on partial input and on Ugandan place names:
#      typing "kyen" offers Kyengera, where OpenStreetMap often needs the whole
#      word. It also ranks by prominence, so the suburb somebody means comes
#      first rather than a road with a similar name.
#
#    WHICH APIs TO ENABLE (both, in Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services)
#      • Places API (New)  — the as-you-type suggestions. This is the one that
#        matters, and it must be the NEW one: the legacy Places Autocomplete is
#        closed to projects created after March 2025.
#      • Geocoding API     — turns the chosen place into coordinates, and is the
#        fallback if Places is not enabled.
#      With only Geocoding on, suggestions still work but are noticeably worse
#      on partial words. With neither, the shop falls back to OpenStreetMap.
#
#    THE COST, PLAINLY
#      Google Maps Platform requires a billing account with a card on file, even
#      to stay inside the free monthly allowance — that is the part worth
#      knowing before you start. Usage here is small by construction: the
#      browser waits 300ms between keystrokes, results are cached at the CDN for
#      an hour, and coordinates are fetched once when a shopper PICKS a place
#      rather than on every suggestion. Check the current free tier and set a
#      budget cap in Google Cloud before enabling anything.
#
#      If you would rather not have a card on file at all, leave this blank. The
#      checkout keeps working exactly as it does today.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=


# ── PESAPAL: NOT SET HERE ANY MORE ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Card and mobile money now run entirely inside WordPress, in the Kandi Pesapal
# plugin. The keys live at wp-admin → Kandi Storefront → Pesapal, and that
# screen has a "Test Pesapal connection" button that says exactly which step
# fails when something is wrong.
#
# They moved because calling Pesapal from a serverless function on this host
# killed the function before it could answer: the shopper saw a bare 502 with
# no message and nothing was written to any log. WordPress is an ordinary PHP
# process with no execution ceiling and a readable log, it already holds the
# order being paid for, and Pesapal's IPN can call it directly.
#
# The four variables below are a fallback for a deployment whose WordPress does
# not have the Kandi Pesapal plugin yet. If yours does — and it should — leave
# them empty or delete them; anything set here is ignored.
#
PESAPAL_CONSUMER_KEY=
PESAPAL_CONSUMER_SECRET=
PESAPAL_ENV=sandbox
PESAPAL_IPN_ID=


# ── HARDENING: STRONGLY RECOMMENDED BEFORE A REAL LAUNCH ────────────────────
#
# None of the five blocks below is required to run the shop, and every one of
# them changes what an attacker can do to it. They are listed in the order
# worth setting them.
#
# ── DOES ANY OF THIS COST MONEY? NO ────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   Upstash Redis     Free tier. Enough commands per day for a shop this size
#                     several times over. No card to sign up.
#   Turnstile         Free, with no paid tier to be upsold to — Cloudflare
#                     built it as the alternative to reCAPTCHA.
#   Session key       A random string you generate. Costs nothing, needs no
#   Checkout key      account, and takes one command each — see the blocks.
#   OneSignal         Free for unlimited mobile push, which is the only thing
#                     this shop uses it for. Details in block 5.
#
# Every one of them also has a free-forever tier rather than a trial, so none of
# this becomes a bill in thirty days. If a service ever does start asking for a
# card, delete the keys from this file: each block is written so that the shop
# keeps working without it, and says in its own text exactly what it loses.


# 1. SHARED RATE LIMITING AND IDEMPOTENCY  (Upstash Redis)
#
#    Without these, both the rate limiter (`lib/rate-limit.ts`) and the
#    idempotency store (`lib/idempotency.ts`) keep their state in one server
#    process's memory. That has two honest limits: on a serverless deployment
#    each warm instance counts separately, so the effective limit is the
#    configured one times the number of instances; and a cold start forgets
#    everything.
#
#    It catches the double tap and the browser retry — both land on the same
#    warm instance seconds apart — and it does not reliably catch a shopper
#    whose connection stalled and who retried a minute later against a
#    different instance. That case creates a duplicate order.
#
#    Set these and both become shared and durable. Free tier at
#    https://console.upstash.com — create a Redis database, copy the two REST
#    values off its dashboard.
#
#    This is NOT a substitute for a WAF. Cloudflare's or Vercel's own rate
#    limiting in front of the origin stops a flood before it costs a function
#    invocation; this stops what gets through.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=


# 2. BOT PROTECTION ON CHECKOUT  (Cloudflare Turnstile)
#
#    Checkout creates real WooCommerce orders from an unauthenticated POST.
#    Until both of these are set there is no bot check on it — only the rate
#    limit and the item caps.
#
#    Cloudflare dashboard → Turnstile → Add site, for this shop's domain. Use
#    the "Managed" mode: most shoppers never see a challenge at all, which
#    matters on the one screen where an interruption costs a sale.
#
#    The site key is public and ships in the browser bundle; that is what it is
#    for. The secret key is server-only — never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
#    If Cloudflare is unreachable the server lets the request through rather
#    than failing the sale; see `lib/turnstile.ts` for why.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=


# 3. SESSION ENCRYPTION  (owner sign-in)
#
#    The owner's session cookie is AES-256-GCM ciphertext with a twelve-hour
#    expiry sealed inside it. It used to be the owner passcode in plain text,
#    valid for thirty days — see `lib/sealed-cookie.ts`.
#
#    Unset, the key is derived from KANDI_API_SECRET, which works. Setting a
#    separate one is better: a session key and a backend key should not be able
#    to compromise each other. Long and random, like KANDI_API_SECRET.
#
#    Rotating this signs every owner out immediately, which is the only
#    revocation this scheme has. Worth knowing before you need it.
KANDI_SESSION_SECRET=


# 4. PAYMENT AUTHORISATION KEY
#
#    Signs the token that proves whoever is starting a payment is the person
#    who placed the order. Without one, `/api/payments/pesapal/start` took a
#    bare WooCommerce order id — and those are sequential, so a loop could open
#    a payment against any order in the shop and read that buyer's name, email,
#    phone and address out of the reply. See `lib/checkout-token.ts`.
#
#    Unset, it falls back to KANDI_API_SECRET, so payments keep working with no
#    action needed. A separate key is better for the same reason as above.
KANDI_CHECKOUT_SECRET=


# 5. PUSH NOTIFICATIONS (OneSignal)
#
#    What they do: the app on a shopper's phone buzzes when their order is
#    confirmed, is out for delivery, arrives or is cancelled, and a seller's
#    phone buzzes when an order lands for them to pack or a payout goes out.
#
#    ---- THIS IS FREE, AND HERE IS WHY IT STAYS FREE ----
#
#    OneSignal charges for THREE products: email, SMS, and web push above
#    10,000 subscribers. Mobile push — an app on a phone, which is the only
#    thing this shop sends — is unlimited on the free plan and has been for
#    years. There is no card at sign-up.
#
#    The shop cannot accidentally wander into a paid product, and that is
#    enforced in code rather than by being careful: every send in lib/push.ts
#    hardcodes target_channel: "push", and there is no OneSignal email or SMS
#    call anywhere in the repository. Shop email goes through wp_mail on
#    WordPress instead — see the Kandi Notifications plugin — so it never
#    touches OneSignal's billed email product at all.
#
#    The one thing to watch: if you later add a web-push prompt to the website
#    (as opposed to the app), browser subscribers count toward the 10,000 free
#    web tier. Nothing here does that today.
#
#    ---- HOW TO GET THE TWO KEYS (about five minutes) ----
#
#      1. onesignal.com → Sign up free. Email and a password; no card.
#      2. It asks you to create an app. Name it "Kandi" and pick your
#         organisation.
#      3. Choose a platform: "Google Android (FCM)" for the Play Store build,
#         "Apple iOS (APNs)" if you ship on iPhone. Android is the one that
#         matters for this market; you can add iOS later without redoing this.
#         Android asks you to upload a Firebase key — that is Google's console,
#         also free, and OneSignal walks you through it on that screen.
#      4. Left sidebar → Settings → Keys & IDs.
#      5. Copy "OneSignal App ID" into ONESIGNAL_APP_ID below.
#         Copy "REST API Key" into ONESIGNAL_REST_API_KEY below.
#      6. Redeploy the storefront so it picks the new values up.
#      7. In WordPress: WooCommerce → Kandi Notifications → "Send a test
#         notification". It tells you in plain words whether it connected, and
#         which of the four possible things is missing if it did not.
#
#    The App ID must be the SAME one the Flutter app is built with, or the app's
#    subscriptions land in a different OneSignal project and nothing arrives.
#    The REST key is SERVER ONLY — note that neither of these has a NEXT_PUBLIC_
#    prefix, which is what keeps it out of the browser bundle. Anyone holding
#    that key can send notifications to every one of your users.
#
#    ---- LEAVING THEM BLANK IS SAFE, AND IS THE DEFAULT ----
#
#    Every push call becomes a no-op that reports it did nothing, and
#    /api/notifications/send answers 200 with skipped:true. A shop with no push
#    configured still takes orders, still sends every email, and never errors.
#    Push is the extra channel, not one anything depends on.
#
#    ---- THE OTHER HALF IS IN WORDPRESS ----
#
#    Nothing here fires on its own. WordPress is what knows an order moved, so
#    Kandi Notifications posts to /api/notifications/send when it does — which
#    needs the storefront URL known to WordPress (Kandi Storefront → Settings)
#    and KANDI_API_SECRET matching on both sides.
ONESIGNAL_APP_ID=
ONESIGNAL_REST_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PHONE VERIFICATION — EgoSMS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#    A 6-digit code stands in front of the first checkout, account creation and
#    seller sign-up. Every order here is completed by a rider ringing the
#    number on it and most are cash on delivery, so a number nobody holds is a
#    packed parcel and a wasted trip. See docs/phone-verification.md.
#
#    ---- WHY EGOSMS AND NOT TWILIO ----
#
#    Twilio Verify bills about $0.05 PER VERIFICATION on top of the message —
#    roughly UGX 190 a signup before an SMS is sent — and routes Uganda traffic
#    through an international aggregator, so the message costs several times the
#    local rate as well. EgoSMS is Ugandan, pay-as-you-go, has no
#    per-verification fee, and needs no SDK: one JSON POST to
#    https://comms.egosms.co/api/v1/json/
#
#    ---- GETTING THE THREE VALUES ----
#
#    Sign up at egosms.co, top up over mobile money, then:
#      KANDI_SMS_USERNAME   your EgoSMS account username
#      KANDI_SMS_PASSWORD   the API key from the dashboard, NOT the password you
#                           log in with
#      KANDI_SMS_SENDER_ID  the sender name recipients see. It must be
#                           REGISTERED with EgoSMS first — an unregistered
#                           sender id is accepted by the API and then not
#                           delivered, which looks exactly like the code never
#                           arriving.
#
#    ---- LEAVING THEM BLANK ----
#
#    In development the code is printed to the server console instead of being
#    sent, so the whole flow can be tested before the account exists. In
#    PRODUCTION a blank gateway is a failure, deliberately: a live shop must not
#    wave every shopper past a check that is not running.
KANDI_SMS_USERNAME=
KANDI_SMS_PASSWORD=
KANDI_SMS_SENDER_ID=KandiUg

# 2. Email codes — NOTHING TO SET.
#
#    Every SMS is real money; an email code is not, so the dialog offers "use my
#    email instead" as a first-class option. It goes through WordPress own
#    mailer, so it arrives from the same address as every order confirmation,
#    through whatever SMTP the host is already set up with. No second sending
#    domain, no second reputation to keep clean, no second bill.
#
#    Requires kandi-customer-auth.php at a version that has the
#    /customers/otp-mail route. If email codes do not arrive, the fault is the
#    site mail configuration -- the same one that would stop password resets.

# 3. Seals the codes and the 90-day "this browser is verified" cookie.
#
#    Falls back to KANDI_API_SECRET when unset, so verification works out of the
#    box. Setting a dedicated value is better — two secrets that cannot
#    compromise each other — and rotating it signs every verified browser out at
#    once.
KANDI_SESSION_SECRET=
